keep-data: the old system Ubuntu 22.04.5 LTS (btr) on /dev/vda2, subvolume @; UEFI firmware; the layout of the profile is not applied, no disk is wiped or partitioned
FORMAT (what is on them is lost)
  /dev/vda2  btrfs  /  (the subvolume @ is deleted and made anew; the rest of the file system stays)
KEEP (as they are, mounted where they were)
  /dev/vda1  vfat   /boot/efi  (the EFI system partition: not formatted, the new boot loader is added)
  /dev/vda2  btrfs  /home  (the subvolume @home of the root's btrfs)
  /dev/vda2  btrfs  /.snapshots  (the subvolume @snapshots of the root's btrfs)
LOST (on the old root, beyond the system itself)
  /etc  6M
  /var/lib/machines  879M
WARNINGS
  /dev/vda2:@snapshots/1/snapshot (Ubuntu 22.04.5 LTS) holds an operating system's files too, a copy or snapshot whose fstab names another root: left as it is
  /dev/vda2:@snapshots/2/snapshot (Ubuntu 22.04.5 LTS) holds an operating system's files too, a copy or snapshot whose fstab names another root: left as it is
  the subvolumes inside the root subvolume go with it: @/var/lib/machines
  the root subvolume @ holds the default subvolume of /dev/vda2, which cannot be deleted: the default is the top of the file system while the root subvolume is replaced, and stays that
  /home is kept: the profile's users get their home directories and keys there; a home directory that is there already goes to the user of its name (its files change owner where the user's ID differs), its authorized_keys is replaced (the old one saved beside it)
